3. How much should Freedom World receive?

I agree with your underlying principle: the creators should receive the larger share because they’re doing most of the work.

For planning purposes, I’d explore a model in which Freedom World receives 10–15% of defined net receipts, with the creator team receiving the remaining 85–90%.

These are illustrative starting points for negotiation, not finalized terms.

For example, consider a hypothetical game that generates $100,000 in eligible net receipts.

Distribution	Creator team: 90%	Freedom World: 10%
Eligible net receipts	$100,000	$100,000
Allocation	$90,000	$10,000

In this illustration, the creator team receives $90,000 and Freedom World receives $10,000.

But we must define net receipts carefully.

It could mean money actually received after specified deductions, such as platform fees, refunds, chargebacks, and applicable transaction taxes. Development expenses, publisher advances, marketing costs, and other deductions would need separate treatment.

We should also distinguish a percentage of net receipts from a percentage of profit. They’re not the same thing.

My recommendation

Start by modeling a 90/10 arrangement, then test whether it can support Freedom World’s ongoing hosting, AI infrastructure, community operations, and creator support.

We should also consider different arrangements for different situations.

For example:

* A game created independently by a team using general-purpose tools.
* A game receiving substantial, project-specific funding from Freedom World.
* A game receiving additional investment for marketing or production.
* A game that incorporates contributions from several independent teams.

Those situations may justify different terms, provided the differences are explained and agreed upon before the relevant work begins.

We should not charge the same rate automatically when the amount of support and investment differs substantially.

