4. How do we stop our best games from disappearing?

This is an important concern, and I wouldn’t try to solve it by preventing creators from leaving.

I’d solve it by making staying on Freedom World valuable.

The Freedom World Continuity Agreement

For games developed through the partnership program, the agreement could establish the following:

1. Continued availability

The game remains available on Freedom World under the agreed hosting and distribution terms, including any provisions for outages, discontinuation, or exceptional circumstances.

2. Continuing economic participation

Freedom World receives the agreed share of covered revenue from specified commercial exploitation of the original game.

3. Outside publishing rights

Creators can seek outside publishers and release the game on other platforms under the agreed licensing and economic arrangements.

4. Creator independence

The team can establish a studio, develop new products, hire additional contributors, and pursue opportunities beyond Freedom World.

5. Protection of original work

The agreement distinguishes Freedom World’s platform assets, the team’s original intellectual property, third-party assets, and any jointly developed material.

This last point matters enormously.

If a team creates a new fantasy game after leaving, it should generally be free to build another fantasy game. Freedom World should not attempt to own an entire genre or prevent people from using general skills they learned while participating.

The agreement can protect specific intellectual property and contractual rights without restricting legitimate future creativity.

And if a creator wants to take the original game outside Freedom World, the licensing and commercial terms should already explain how that works.

